# Construction qualification tools

These tools qualify recorded original-parser and public Rust captures for the assigned finite construction family. They preserve discrepancies and report required missing evidence as `OPEN`; they do not grant feature acceptance. Existing construction captures, corpus, source and receipts remain read-only inputs.

The coordinator first executes the original-method oracle against the pinned bundle and the unchanged schema-1 corpus, then compares all captures:

```text
node reference_expectations.cjs ABS_BUNDLE ABS_CORPUS ABS_NEW_REFERENCE_OUTPUT
python3 compare_construction.py --corpus ABS_CORPUS --native ABS_NATIVE --rust ABS_RUST --references ABS_REFERENCE_OUTPUT --out ABS_NEW_REPORT --self-test
```

Both outputs must be new files on `/workspace`. The comparator writes its full report before returning exit 1 when differences, missing required input or named OPEN obligations remain. Input hashes bind the report to supplied bytes. The coordinator must also retain execution receipts, actual candidate/source/dependency bindings and resulting report hashes; hashes and provenance labels alone do not prove execution or native artifact correspondence.

The new oracle uses the unmodified pinned-bundle prefix before the unique CLI boundary and the original empty-filesystem service factory/parser. It calls original `streamReferences` on actual parsed nodes and custom `AstReflection.getReferenceType` on each real reference info, including the actual chain index. It also calls original `getTypeMetaData` and `findNodesForProperty`; expected target types and property spans are never inferred from Rust. The property helper reads CST element associations and grammar containers. AST reference `ref`, `error` and `$nodeDescription` getters remain unevaluated. Property-helper exceptions, unavailable reference spans and parser capture failures remain explicit.

Reference output is `{cases: [{id, source, references, property_spans, metadata, aliases, ...}], provenance}`. Reference identities are `(owner_path, property, index)`, with `index: null` for a scalar reference. Entries retain raw reference text, original target type, source CST span and own-key inventory. Property-span entries retain every original helper result or its exception. Metadata retains original mandatory names/kinds, own keys and explicit `$childIndex` presence/value. Structural paths use escaped property segments and ordered array indices; cache fields never mint containment identities.

The comparator builds separate native-object/public-Rust-ID path maps and requires a bijection through every structural field. It checks node types, all semantic field presence and typed values, array order, cache aliases, container parent/property/index presence, mandatory array/boolean kinds, origins and source/CST-backed Datum lexemes. It validates Rust byte and UTF-16 slices against the exact source, compares reference target types with original method results, and checks recapture inventories and runtime/corpus identity. Boolean/number coercion, absent/undefined/null substitution, signed-zero/NaN changes and reference-target guesses are not accepted. The self-test makes eight deliberately divergent complete case captures covering types, cache aliases, order, containers, origins, reference targets, Datum spans and absent fields.

Native `$childIndex` values without public Rust counterparts remain per-node OPEN findings. Full CST topology, line/range/grammar-feature retarget identities, JS descriptors/insertion order, unevaluated reference/link state, external metamodel/document fields and private owner lifecycle are separately named OPEN. Raw native inventories and graphs remain available; those boundaries are not silently passed or projected away. Datum lexemes with missing or ambiguous original CST assignments also remain OPEN. The tool must not describe the old structural inspector's 79 cache discrepancies as the complete set of possible native differences.

Finite, malformed and outside-whitelist cases remain separate. A finite refusal or native parse failure is a discrepancy. For other categories the declared Rust error family is checked, while actual native diagnostics and the Rust refusal remain explicit required domain/diagnostic dispositions. Native success is never converted into matching Rust success by relabeling an unsupported construct. Recovered ASTs do not become accepted models.

The VM executes trusted pinned code and permits builtin module imports; it is not an OS I/O/security sandbox. The original empty filesystem is supplied without a connection, server or workspace initialization, and no link/build/validation workflow is invoked. Runtime/bundle/executable provenance supports only the recorded current Node/V8. Deployed Docker Node20, whole-artifact/source correspondence, cancellation/resource qualification, importer/UI behavior, persistence and full acceptance remain separate gates. This source preparation contains no executed result.
